From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA: sm_121 wide row block for the dense Q6_K matvecs

Half of this model's decode token is dense Q6_K matvec. A byte census of the
21-patch frontier build, taken from the GGUF tensor table and matched launch
for launch against an nsys decode trace, reads 2317 MB of matvec weights per
token, of which the Q6_K dense group is 1449 MB: attn_qkv x30 and attn_q x10
at [2048 x 8192] (550 MB), the [2048 x 248320] output head (417 MB), ssm_out
x30 and attn_output x10 at [4096 x 2048] (275 MB) and attn_gate x30 at
[2048 x 4096] (206 MB).

That group runs at 202-214 GB/s. The question this patch answers is what the
number would be if nothing were wrong, and the answer comes from a
profiler-free probe rather than from the datasheet. A CUDA program that
reproduces the output head's exact launch geometry - 248320 blocks, one
1680-byte row of DRAM each, cudaEvent timing, pseudo-random contents - reads

    64 threads/block   227.2 232.5 231.1 232.2 232.3  GB/s   (median 231.8)
   128 threads/block   240.3 242.5 243.0 242.7 243.0  GB/s   (median 242.7)
   256 threads/block   243.7 244.1 244.1 244.3 244.7  GB/s   (median 244.1)

with an L2-residency control at the same geometry (13.8 MB, inside the 24 MB
L2) reading 1306-1363 GB/s, i.e. 5.6x, so the 417 MB figures are DRAM and not
cache. The kernel runs the 64-thread column and the DRAM will serve the
128-thread one. Q6_K has vdr = 1 and a 210-byte block, so a lane can only
issue narrow loads and no wider load exists (measured dead twice on this
track); the remaining way to get more of them in flight against one row is
more lanes on the row.

The SM121 mmvq table gives K-quants two warps. This gives Q6_K four, and only
Q6_K:

    if (sm121_wide_row_block(type, ncols_dst, table_id)) return 4;

rows_per_block does not move. calc_rows_per_block returns 1 for every
ncols_dst == 1 shape that is not small_k, and Q6_K never takes the small_k
path on this model, so the grid is unchanged and only the block grows. That is
what separates this from the whole-table nwarps sweep this track recorded as
neutral: raising nwarps for the entire K-quant row also raised the expert
shapes to rows_per_block = 2*nwarps = 8, which the same ledger separately
measured at -0.4%, so the two effects were being netted against each other in
one number.

Firing proof, nsys node-graph census, grids identical and blocks doubled:

    [2048 x 248320] head      grid 248320x1x1  blk 32x2 -> 32x4   2044 -> 1977 us
    [4096 x 2048] ssm_out     grid   2048x1x1  blk 32x2 -> 32x4     36.99 -> 35.26 us
    [2048 x 8192] qkv/q       grid   8192x1x1  blk 32x2 -> 32x4     66.50 -> 66.61 us
    [2048 x 4096] attn_gate   grid   4096x1x1  blk 32x2 -> 32x4     33.54 -> 33.73 us

and every Q4_K, Q5_K, Q8_0 and F32 launch unchanged in grid and block.

Measurement. Two independently built shared libraries, distinct md5, selected
by LD_LIBRARY_PATH; counterbalanced arm order; whole-process launches;
llama-bench tg128 -r 2.

    session A, 6 rounds   ctl 75.92-76.13   wide 76.64-77.10   median ratio 1.0123
    session B, 6 rounds   ctl 75.91-76.15   wide 76.93-77.14   median ratio 1.0131
    session C, 5 rounds   ctl 75.90-76.09   wide 76.76-76.92   median ratio 1.0120

17 of 17 rounds disjoint. pp512 overlaps in both directions across 6 rounds
(2339-2355 against 2347-2358), so prefill is neutral - decode-shape mmvq is
not on the prefill path, which runs MMQ.

Eight warps was swept in the same harness and is an occupancy cliff: 72.73-72.93
against the control's 75.91-76.15, -4.2%. Four is the setting.

Perplexity is unchanged at the gate shape: -c 512 --chunks 8 on the held-out
corpus reads 3.9306 on five consecutive loads of the wide build and 3.9306 on
five of the control, equal to the parent frontier's recorded value.

GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_SM121_WIDE_ROW_BLOCK restores the two-warp block.
---
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu
index 0291459..1ed84a9 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu
@@ -155,6 +155,34 @@ static constexpr __host__ __device__ bool sm121_one_warp_row(
 #endif
 }
 
+// sm_121 wide row block. The dense Q6_K matvecs are half of this model's decode
+// token: attn_qkv/attn_q [2048 x 8192], attn_gate [2048 x 4096], ssm_out and
+// attn_output [4096 x 2048] and the [2048 x 248320] output head, 1449 MB of the
+// 2317 MB of weights a token reads. Q6_K has vdr = 1 and a 210-byte block, so a
+// lane can only issue narrow loads; the way to get more of them in flight per
+// row is more lanes on the row, not wider ones. A profiler-free probe of the
+// output head's exact geometry - 248320 blocks, one 1680-byte row each - reads
+// 231 GB/s at 64 threads per block and 243 GB/s at 128, while the kernel itself
+// runs at 203 GB/s, so the block is the narrower of the two constraints. The
+// K-quant entry of the SM121 table gives two warps; Q6_K takes four. Eight was
+// swept and is an occupancy cliff (-4.2% decode).
+//
+// rows_per_block is unaffected: calc_rows_per_block returns 1 for every
+// ncols_dst == 1 shape that is not small_k, and Q6_K never takes the small_k
+// path on this model, so the grid is unchanged and only the block grows. That
+// is what separates this from the earlier whole-table nwarps sweep, which moved
+// the expert shapes to rows_per_block = 2*nwarps = 8 at the same time and paid
+// that back against itself.
+static constexpr __host__ __device__ bool sm121_wide_row_block(
+        ggml_type type, int ncols_dst, mmvq_parameter_table_id table_id) {
+#ifdef GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_SM121_WIDE_ROW_BLOCK
+    (void) type; (void) ncols_dst; (void) table_id;
+    return false;
+#else
+    return table_id == MMVQ_PARAMETERS_SM121 && ncols_dst == 1 && type == GGML_TYPE_Q6_K;
+#endif
+}
+
 // sm_121 mmvq k-loop unroll factor. On this device the dominant decode kernels
 // are the Q6_K dense matvecs (~50% of decode kernel time in an nsys census):
 // Q6_K has vdr = 1 and its 210-byte block forbids any wider load, so a lane
@@ -498,6 +526,9 @@ static constexpr __host__ __device__ int calc_nwarps(ggml_type type, int ncols_d
             if (sm121_one_warp_row(type, ncols_dst, table_id)) {
                 return 1;
             }
+            if (sm121_wide_row_block(type, ncols_dst, table_id)) {
+                return 4;
+            }
             switch (type) {
                 case GGML_TYPE_Q2_K:
                 case GGML_TYPE_Q3_K:
